              "tellMeMore": "The cell theory Robert Hooke was an English sci- entist who lived at the same time as van Leeuwenhoek. Hooke used a compound light microscope to study cork, the dead cells of oak bark. In cork, Hooke observed small geomet- ric shapes, like those shown in Figure 7.1. Hooke gave these box- shaped structures the name cells because they reminded him of the small rooms monks lived in at a monastery. Cells are the basic units of all living things. Several scientists extended Hooke\u2019s observations and drew some impor- tant conclusions. In the 1830s, the German scientist Matthias Schleiden observed a variety of plants and concluded that all plants are com- posed of cells. Another German sci- entist, Theodor Schwann, made sim- ilar observations on animals.",
