              "tellMeMore": "12.1 MENDELIAN INHERITANCE OF HUMAN TRAITS 311 that the ratio of homozygous domi- nant to heterozygous to homozygous recessive genotypes among their chil- dren would be 1:2:1. Of those geno- types possible for the members of generation II, only the homozygous recessive genotype will express the trait, which is the case for II-3. Y ou can\u2019t tell the genotypes of II-4 and II-5, but they have a normal phe- notype. If you look at the Punnett square you made, you can see that the probability that II-4 and II-5 are carriers is two in three for each because they can have only two possible geno- types\u2014homozygous normal and het- erozygous. The homozygous recessive genotype is not a possibility in these individuals because neither of them shows the affected phenotype. Because none of the children in generation III are affected and because the recessive allele is rare in the gen- eral population, it is reasonably safe to assume that II-1 is not a carrier. Y ou know that II-2 must be a carrier like her parents because she has passed on the recessive allele to subsequent gen- eration IV . Because III-1 has one par- ent who is heterozygous and the other parent who is assumed to be homozy- gous n",
