              "tellMeMore": "Chapter 12 303 family. Alanguage familyis a group of related lan- guages that developed from an earlier language. The Indo-European family has several major branches in Europe. Most people in eastern Europe speak Slavic languages\u2014including Bulgarian, Czech, Polish, Slovak, Ukrainian, Belorussian, and Serbo- Croatian\u2014or Baltic languages, such as Latvian and Lithuanian. In northern Europe, most people speak Germanic languages\u2013\u2013German, Dutch, English, Danish, Swedish, and Norwegian. The Romance languages, which come from Latin, the language of the Roman Empire, are widely spoken in southern Europe. They include Italian, Spanish, Portuguese, French, and Romanian. Other Indo-European branches are Greek, Albanian, and the Celtic lan- guages.",
