              "tellMeMore": "764 Unit 10 for regional unity. In 1992 they agreed to establish a free-trade area and to reduce tariffs on nonagricul- tural products by 2008. Transportation Southeast Asia\u2019s peninsulas, islands, long coast- lines, and many rivers make water transportation the most common way to move people and goods in the region. However, rain-swollen rivers in the trop- ical forests sometimes make travel slow and diffi- cult. In some remote areas, such as Indonesia\u2019s territory of Irian Jaya, people receive supplies by air as well as by water. Southeast Asia has long been the crossroads of major ocean trade routes. Today most shipping between Europe and East Asia passes through the Strait of Malacca, near Singapore. This transporta- tion \u201cchoke point,\u201d or strategic location, enables Singapore to prosper as a free port, a place where goods can be unloaded, stored, and reshipped free of import duties.",
